{
 "id": "vision_onoff",
 "version": "1.0.0",
 "depends_on": [
  "vision_waves"
 ],
 "description": "RUNG 6 \u2014 THE SIGNED SIMPLE LAYER via an ON/OFF RETINAL SPLIT. Rung 5 measured why the Units have no orientation-selective RESPONSE (OSI 0.004 vs an ideal Gabor's 1.000): Oja on non-negative input keeps weights strictly positive (frac(w<0)=0.0000), so the response is DC-dominated and the oriented structure that IS in the weights never reaches it (GABOR vs GABOR+DC: identical anisotropy 0.8672, OSI 1.0000 vs 0.0000). Biology does not force the sign into the WEIGHT \u2014 it splits the RETINA into ON and OFF pathways and keeps synapses sign-constant (Dale), so the effective RF w+ - w- is signed while every weight stays >= 0. One wave field drives BOTH channels (stage II cholinergic waves are not segregated; Gjorgjieva 2009 has burst-timing driving ON/OFF segregation), so aligned adjacent ON/OFF subregions (Hubel & Wiesel; Reid & Alonso) are an OUTCOME to grow, never an input. Learning is Miller & MacKay's subtractive normalisation, the canonical rule for exactly this: it projects out the uniform/DC mode that multiplicative/divisive normalisation (our Oja == GCAL's, to first order) preserves. onoff=0 by DEFAULT so every committed result reproduces byte-identically.",
 "microtheory": {
  "name": "vision_onoff",
  "description": "the sign comes from the PATHWAY, not the weight: one wave field -> two non-negative retinal channels (ON: x, OFF: 1-x) -> a cortical cell with non-negative weights whose EFFECTIVE receptive field w+ - w- is signed; subtractive normalisation projects out the DC mode so the signed, oriented mode is the only one that can grow."
 },
 "nodes": [
  {
   "local_id": 0,
   "type": "OnOffParams",
   "attrs": {
    "name": "default",
    "onoff": 0.0,
    "split": 1.0,
    "subnorm": 1.0,
    "frozen": 0.0,
    "oo_eta": 0.0005,
    "oo_wmax": 2.0,
    "theta_rate": 0.05,
    "homeo": 1.0,
    "comment": "ALL knobs are GRAPH DATA (PROTOCOL 0) \u2014 every ablation is the agent's own configuration, never a Python code path. onoff: 0 (DEFAULT) = the layer is inert, so every committed result reproduces byte-identically (PROTOCOL 9). split: 1 = OFF cells invert (x_OFF = 1-x), the SIGNED pathway; 0 = OFF behaves as ON (x_OFF = x) = a SINGLE non-negative channel = rung 5's condition. The control changes ONLY the sign \u2014 identical anatomy, identical synapse count, identical rules \u2014 because rung 4 learned the hard way that an ablation which also removes anatomy is not null. subnorm: 1 = Miller & MacKay subtractive normalisation; 0 = plain Oja (predicted to collapse to the uniform mode). frozen: 1 = A REAL FREEZE gating every learning rule here. THE BUG IT FIXES: `oja=0` IS NOT A FREEZE \u2014 bh_potentiate / bh_depress / bh_scale are each gated on WaveParams.oja==0.0, so oja=0 switches the OTHER learning rule ON (measured: |dw| up to 47.9 across a single probe, driving three different hand-set poolings to one attractor). A probe must never train what it measures. oo_eta: 5e-4, the regime rung 2's Oja check measured as stable (2e-3 diverges; the eta*y^2 < 1 bound). theta_rate: the EMA timescale of the spike threshold (theta <- theta + theta_rate*(act - theta)), a timescale, not a tuned optimum. homeo: 1 = the threshold adapts; 0 = it is pinned (the no-threshold arm \u2014 measured to give the ideal split-Gabor OSI 0.0006, i.e. no selectivity at all). oo_wmax: the saturating upper bound. In Miller's model the bounds are not a safety rail \u2014 saturation is the symmetry-breaking that makes subregions SEGREGATE rather than rest at the mean."
   }
  }
 ],
